1
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:03,680
Antarctica, 2009.

2
00:00:04,390 --> 00:00:07,470
A Weddell seal lies on
a small raft of ice.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:10,850
Around it, the water is moving.

4
00:00:11,978 --> 00:00:16,358
A group of killer whales swims
side by side towards the floe.

5
00:00:17,028 --> 00:00:22,748
At the last moment, they dive together,
and a wave rolls across the ice.

6
00:00:23,841 --> 00:00:25,601
The seal slides into the sea.

7
00:00:26,371 --> 00:00:28,131
It should be the end of the story.

8
00:00:30,248 --> 00:00:34,658
But a few metres away, a humpback
whale rolls onto its back.

9
00:00:35,398 --> 00:00:40,428
The seal is swept up onto its huge
chest, between its two long flippers.

10
00:00:41,506 --> 00:00:46,186
When the seal begins to slip, the
whale lifts it back with a flipper.

11
00:00:46,856 --> 00:00:52,216
A few moments later, the seal swims
to another floe, out of reach.

12
00:00:53,108 --> 00:00:56,578
Two scientists in a small
boat watched the whole scene.

13
00:00:57,368 --> 00:00:59,718
They could hardly believe
what they had seen.

14
00:01:00,712 --> 00:01:02,392
Because it makes no sense.

15
00:01:05,358 --> 00:01:07,948
A humpback whale does not eat seals.

16
00:01:08,748 --> 00:01:12,388
It does not need them, and
it does not know them.

17
00:01:13,088 --> 00:01:17,278
Yet it had just put itself between a
pack of killer whales and their prey.

18
00:01:18,398 --> 00:01:20,438
And it was not the only one.

19
00:01:28,011 --> 00:01:32,861
To understand this story,
start with the whale itself.

20
00:01:33,908 --> 00:01:36,395
An adult humpback can be around fifteen

21
00:01:36,445 --> 00:01:39,308
metres long and weigh
thirty tonnes or more.

22
00:01:40,058 --> 00:01:43,588
That is heavier than five
adult orcas put together.

23
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:49,520
Its scientific name,
Megaptera, means big wings.

24
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:55,190
Its flippers can be up to five metres
long, almost a third of its body.

25
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:58,160
No other whale has flippers like them.

26
00:01:59,126 --> 00:02:01,811
The edges of those flippers are covered

27
00:02:01,861 --> 00:02:04,596
in bumps, and often in sharp barnacles.

28
00:02:05,346 --> 00:02:07,856
A blow from one of them can cut skin.

29
00:02:08,932 --> 00:02:12,562
Its tail, the fluke, can
be five metres wide.

30
00:02:13,232 --> 00:02:17,962
Each tail has its own pattern of
black and white, like a fingerprint.

31
00:02:18,971 --> 00:02:21,501
Humpbacks eat small fish and krill.

32
00:02:22,221 --> 00:02:26,630
They spend their summers feeding in
cold waters, then travel thousands of

33
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,001
kilometres to warm seas to give birth.

34
00:02:30,181 --> 00:02:32,731
In the breeding season, males sing.

35
00:02:33,501 --> 00:02:37,483
Their songs can last for twenty
minutes, and be repeated for hours,

36
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:40,061
carrying for kilometres through the water.

37
00:02:41,138 --> 00:02:44,378
They are gentle giants, slow and patient.

38
00:02:45,138 --> 00:02:46,818
But they are not defenceless.

39
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:55,471
On the other side of this story

40
00:02:55,521 --> 00:02:58,630
is the most successful
predator in the ocean.

41
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:04,710
The killer whale, or orca, is not really
a whale but the largest member of

42
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:06,070
the dolphin family.

43
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:10,940
A big male can reach eight or nine
metres and weigh several tonnes.

44
00:03:12,065 --> 00:03:16,955
Orcas live in family groups called
pods, led by older females.

45
00:03:17,725 --> 00:03:20,575
They hunt together, and
they plan together.

46
00:03:21,618 --> 00:03:24,232
Their tactics are passed from mother to

47
00:03:24,282 --> 00:03:27,698
calf, generation after
generation, like a culture.

48
00:03:28,790 --> 00:03:34,278
They see well, hear even better, and
use echolocation, clicks that bounce

49
00:03:34,328 --> 00:03:38,880
off objects, to find their way
and their prey in dark water.

50
00:03:39,957 --> 00:03:43,227
Different pods have their
own sounds, like accents.

51
00:03:44,027 --> 00:03:46,942
Some hunt fish, others hunt seals,

52
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:50,567
others chase sharks or even large whales.

53
00:03:51,647 --> 00:03:53,537
No animal in the sea hunts them.

54
00:03:54,207 --> 00:03:56,777
That is why they are called
the wolves of the sea.

55
00:03:57,843 --> 00:04:00,153
But not all orcas hunt the same thing.

56
00:04:00,933 --> 00:04:03,513
And that detail is the
key to the whole mystery.

57
00:04:11,106 --> 00:04:13,339
In the North Pacific, scientists

58
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:16,456
recognise different
types of killer whales.

59
00:04:17,236 --> 00:04:21,026
They share the same waters,
but they almost never mix.

60
00:04:22,035 --> 00:04:24,705
Some eat only fish, mostly salmon.

61
00:04:25,385 --> 00:04:28,040
They travel in large, noisy family groups

62
00:04:28,090 --> 00:04:30,465
and call to each other all day long.

63
00:04:31,464 --> 00:04:34,986
Others hunt only mammals:
seals, sea lions,

64
00:04:35,036 --> 00:04:38,524
porpoises, and the calves of large whales.

65
00:04:39,324 --> 00:04:42,884
They are often called Bigg's
killer whales, or transients.

66
00:04:43,987 --> 00:04:48,477
Mammal hunters move in small
groups, and they hunt in silence.

67
00:04:49,177 --> 00:04:53,267
Their prey can hear them, so they
say nothing until the attack begins.

68
00:04:54,392 --> 00:04:56,532
And here is the first surprise.

69
00:04:57,272 --> 00:05:00,252
Humpbacks largely ignore
the fish-eating orcas,

70
00:05:00,302 --> 00:05:02,622
even when they swim right past them.

71
00:05:03,612 --> 00:05:08,427
But when mammal-eating orcas start a
hunt, humpbacks often come towards

72
00:05:08,477 --> 00:05:10,532
them, sometimes from far away.

73
00:05:17,098 --> 00:05:22,368
The scientists in that boat in 2009
were Robert Pitman and John Durban.

74
00:05:23,128 --> 00:05:26,778
They were studying killer whales
off the Antarctic Peninsula.

75
00:05:27,805 --> 00:05:32,745
Here, at the bottom of the world,
the sea is full of floating ice.

76
00:05:33,495 --> 00:05:36,472
Seals haul out on the floes to rest,

77
00:05:36,522 --> 00:05:40,475
safe from leopard seals
and orcas in the water.

78
00:05:42,285 --> 00:05:43,785
At least, that is the idea.

79
00:05:44,810 --> 00:05:48,950
The orcas they followed specialise
in seals resting on ice floes.

80
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:52,630
They find a seal, look at it, then work as

81
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:55,590
a team to make a wave that washes it off.

82
00:05:56,697 --> 00:06:00,207
That day, two humpback
whales were swimming nearby.

83
00:06:00,957 --> 00:06:03,042
Each time the orcas closed in, the

84
00:06:03,092 --> 00:06:05,667
humpbacks came between them and the seal.

85
00:06:06,747 --> 00:06:11,385
When the seal ended up on the humpback's
chest, the whale stayed on its

86
00:06:11,435 --> 00:06:16,057
back, with its belly above the water,
holding the seal out of the sea.

87
00:06:17,147 --> 00:06:19,375
The humpbacks were bellowing and slapping

88
00:06:19,425 --> 00:06:21,647
the water with their flippers and tails.

89
00:06:22,447 --> 00:06:25,247
The killer whales gave up and swam away.

90
00:06:26,321 --> 00:06:29,311
Pitman later wrote that
it looked like a rescue.

91
00:06:30,041 --> 00:06:32,861
But one strange event proves nothing.

92
00:06:33,621 --> 00:06:36,461
He wanted to know if it happened again.

93
00:06:44,070 --> 00:06:49,250
Three years later, and half a
world away, it happened again.

94
00:06:50,247 --> 00:06:55,006
Every spring, gray whale mothers swim
north along the coast of California

95
00:06:55,056 --> 00:06:57,509
with their calves, from the lagoons of

96
00:06:57,559 --> 00:07:00,457
Mexico to the feeding
grounds of the Arctic.

97
00:07:01,531 --> 00:07:04,491
Monterey Bay is a
dangerous place for them.

98
00:07:05,151 --> 00:07:07,542
Its deep underwater canyon comes close

99
00:07:07,592 --> 00:07:10,291
to shore, and mammal-eating orcas know it.

100
00:07:11,382 --> 00:07:17,371
In May 2012, in Monterey Bay, California,
a group of mammal-eating orcas

101
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:21,112
separated a gray whale
calf from its mother.

102
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:24,010
The calf did not survive.

103
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:26,370
We will not show that moment.

104
00:07:27,435 --> 00:07:30,295
But what happened next
amazed the people watching.

105
00:07:31,005 --> 00:07:34,445
Humpback whales arrived,
first a pair, then more.

106
00:07:35,441 --> 00:07:38,971
They placed themselves around the
place where the calf had died.

107
00:07:39,661 --> 00:07:44,150
They trumpeted, a loud call blown
through the blowhole, and slapped the

108
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:45,671
water with their tails.

109
00:07:46,770 --> 00:07:49,310
For more than six hours, they stayed.

110
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:53,710
And for all that time, the
orcas had trouble feeding.

111
00:07:54,823 --> 00:07:57,003
The humpbacks could not save the calf.

112
00:07:57,813 --> 00:07:59,333
Yet they did not leave.

113
00:08:00,073 --> 00:08:02,866
To the observer, Alisa
Schulman-Janiger, it

114
00:08:02,916 --> 00:08:05,363
looked like they were standing guard.

115
00:08:12,890 --> 00:08:15,810
Stories like these are hard
to trust on their own.

116
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:19,920
So Robert Pitman and his
colleagues began to collect them.

117
00:08:20,978 --> 00:08:22,707
They gathered reports from

118
00:08:22,757 --> 00:08:26,248
scientists, whale-watching
crews and photographers.

119
00:08:27,018 --> 00:08:29,508
Some went back more than sixty years.

120
00:08:30,566 --> 00:08:32,536
In 2017, they published their results.

121
00:08:33,066 --> 00:08:38,997
They had found one hundred and
fifteen interactions between humpback

122
00:08:39,047 --> 00:08:41,246
whales and killer whales.

123
00:08:42,323 --> 00:08:44,745
In most of them, it was the humpbacks

124
00:08:44,795 --> 00:08:48,203
that approached the orcas,
not the other way round.

125
00:08:49,205 --> 00:08:51,453
And in almost every case where the type

126
00:08:51,503 --> 00:08:54,095
of orca was known, they
were mammal hunters.

127
00:08:54,915 --> 00:08:57,925
The humpbacks were not
reacting to orcas in general.

128
00:08:58,535 --> 00:09:00,115
They were reacting to hunts.

129
00:09:01,214 --> 00:09:03,834
Some of these encounters lasted minutes.

130
00:09:04,514 --> 00:09:06,264
Some lasted for hours.

131
00:09:12,877 --> 00:09:17,687
The study listed at least ten
different prey species that humpbacks

132
00:09:17,737 --> 00:09:20,516
appeared to defend, across the Pacific,

133
00:09:20,566 --> 00:09:23,177
the Atlantic and the Southern Ocean.

134
00:09:24,317 --> 00:09:26,727
Then came the most
surprising result of all.

135
00:09:27,347 --> 00:09:31,507
The scientists looked at what the
orcas had been hunting each time.

136
00:09:32,607 --> 00:09:35,237
Sometimes it was a humpback calf.

137
00:09:36,027 --> 00:09:37,517
But that was rare.

138
00:09:38,607 --> 00:09:40,939
In about nine cases out of ten where

139
00:09:40,989 --> 00:09:44,497
the prey could be identified,
it was another species.

140
00:09:45,606 --> 00:09:46,606
Harbour seals.

141
00:09:47,667 --> 00:09:49,787
California sea lions.

142
00:09:50,781 --> 00:09:54,421
Steller sea lions, gray whale calves,

143
00:09:54,471 --> 00:09:58,161
porpoises, and even an ocean sunfish.

144
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:01,740
A sea lion brings the humpback nothing.

145
00:10:02,470 --> 00:10:04,350
A sunfish brings it nothing.

146
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:07,851
Yet the whales came anyway, and sometimes

147
00:10:07,901 --> 00:10:10,950
they stayed long after
the prey had escaped.

148
00:10:18,407 --> 00:10:23,727
To understand why, look closely at
the underside of a humpback's tail.

149
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:27,370
Many of them carry parallel white lines.

150
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:32,040
These are rake marks, left by
the teeth of killer whales.

151
00:10:33,074 --> 00:10:35,550
In some populations, up to about a

152
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:38,794
third of adult humpbacks
carry these scars.

153
00:10:39,913 --> 00:10:44,601
Most of the scars seem to be made when
the whales are young, often during

154
00:10:44,651 --> 00:10:46,403
their first long migration.

155
00:10:47,493 --> 00:10:50,483
A humpback calf is small,
slow, and far from home.

156
00:10:51,093 --> 00:10:57,853
For mammal-eating orcas, it is one of
the most valuable prey in the ocean.

157
00:10:58,913 --> 00:11:03,079
Off the coast of Western Australia,
scientists have watched mammal-eating

158
00:11:03,129 --> 00:11:05,389
orcas follow humpback mothers and calves

159
00:11:05,439 --> 00:11:07,633
for hours along their migration route.

160
00:11:08,676 --> 00:11:13,626
A mother defends her calf by keeping
it close, often above her own back or

161
00:11:13,676 --> 00:11:18,406
tucked beside her flipper, and turning
her tail towards the attackers.

162
00:11:19,494 --> 00:11:22,154
So every adult humpback alive today has

163
00:11:22,204 --> 00:11:24,914
survived a world full of killer whales.

164
00:11:25,694 --> 00:11:29,604
Many of them have met orcas
up close, and remember it.

165
00:11:36,103 --> 00:11:37,713
A humpback has no teeth.

166
00:11:38,513 --> 00:11:39,743
It cannot bite.

167
00:11:39,953 --> 00:11:43,393
So how does it fight a
predator built to kill?

168
00:11:44,467 --> 00:11:46,829
Watch a humpback at the surface for a

169
00:11:46,879 --> 00:11:49,617
while, and you will see the tools it uses.

170
00:11:50,735 --> 00:11:52,985
Its first weapon is its flippers.

171
00:11:53,765 --> 00:11:58,735
Swung through the water, these five-metre
wings hit with enormous force,

172
00:11:58,785 --> 00:12:01,365
and the barnacles on their edges cut.

173
00:12:02,411 --> 00:12:04,551
Its second weapon is its tail.

174
00:12:05,261 --> 00:12:07,513
One stroke can lift the whole fluke out of

175
00:12:07,563 --> 00:12:09,701
the sea and bring it down with a crash.

176
00:12:10,790 --> 00:12:12,830
Its third weapon is noise.

177
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:16,539
Humpbacks trumpet and
bellow, and those calls

178
00:12:16,589 --> 00:12:19,180
may bring other humpbacks to the scene.

179
00:12:20,132 --> 00:12:22,172
And its last weapon is numbers.

180
00:12:22,922 --> 00:12:25,716
Several humpbacks working
together can surround

181
00:12:25,766 --> 00:12:27,762
the prey and keep the orcas away.

182
00:12:28,844 --> 00:12:31,722
Sometimes an adult male joins a mother

183
00:12:31,772 --> 00:12:34,854
and calf during an attack, as an escort.

184
00:12:35,574 --> 00:12:38,934
Sometimes several unrelated
adults arrive at once.

185
00:12:40,047 --> 00:12:41,917
Orcas are fast and clever.

186
00:12:42,677 --> 00:12:44,781
But a hunt costs them energy, and

187
00:12:44,831 --> 00:12:47,507
a blow from a humpback could injure them.

188
00:12:48,127 --> 00:12:49,847
Often, they simply give up.

189
00:12:57,463 --> 00:12:59,643
So we come back to the big question.

190
00:13:00,403 --> 00:13:03,463
Why would a humpback risk
its safety for a seal?

191
00:13:04,535 --> 00:13:07,665
The first answer is simple:
protecting their own.

192
00:13:08,425 --> 00:13:12,905
Humpbacks protect their calves,
and sometimes the calves of other

193
00:13:12,955 --> 00:13:15,325
humpbacks, which may be relatives.

194
00:13:16,439 --> 00:13:18,539
The second answer is more surprising.

195
00:13:19,239 --> 00:13:22,729
Mammal-eating orcas are silent
until the attack starts.

196
00:13:23,509 --> 00:13:24,949
Then they become loud.

197
00:13:26,057 --> 00:13:28,187
A humpback hearing those sounds from far

198
00:13:28,237 --> 00:13:30,417
away cannot know what is being attacked.

199
00:13:31,147 --> 00:13:32,447
It could be a seal.

200
00:13:33,107 --> 00:13:35,127
Or it could be a humpback calf.

201
00:13:36,256 --> 00:13:41,074
Pitman and his colleagues suggested
that the safest rule is to go and

202
00:13:41,124 --> 00:13:42,606
interfere every time.

203
00:13:43,306 --> 00:13:46,406
If you go too often, you
lose a little energy.

204
00:13:47,166 --> 00:13:51,006
If you stay away when it is
your own kind, you lose a calf.

205
00:13:52,095 --> 00:13:56,355
By this logic, the seal is
saved almost by accident.

206
00:13:57,125 --> 00:14:00,045
The humpback is not thinking
of the seal at all.

207
00:14:00,685 --> 00:14:02,535
It is fighting the killer whale.

208
00:14:10,072 --> 00:14:13,382
But not everyone is
satisfied with that answer.

209
00:14:14,401 --> 00:14:19,011
Other animals help different
species too, sometimes.

210
00:14:19,811 --> 00:14:24,091
Dolphins have been seen staying close
to injured or stranded animals.

211
00:14:24,861 --> 00:14:28,011
But such stories are
rare, and hard to prove.

212
00:14:29,093 --> 00:14:32,149
Remember the whale in Antarctica, lying on

213
00:14:32,199 --> 00:14:35,453
its back, lifting the
seal with its flipper.

214
00:14:36,213 --> 00:14:41,096
Remember the humpbacks in Monterey
Bay, staying for hours beside a calf

215
00:14:41,146 --> 00:14:42,953
they could no longer save.

216
00:14:43,963 --> 00:14:46,933
Humpbacks have large and complex brains.

217
00:14:47,703 --> 00:14:52,171
Male humpbacks sing long songs that
change from year to year and spread

218
00:14:52,221 --> 00:14:54,193
from one population to another.

219
00:14:55,276 --> 00:14:59,837
Some researchers think it is possible
that humpbacks feel something when

220
00:14:59,887 --> 00:15:01,936
they hear an animal in distress.

221
00:15:03,032 --> 00:15:04,902
Pitman himself was careful.

222
00:15:05,542 --> 00:15:07,150
He wrote that when a human

223
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:10,262
protects another species,
we call it compassion.

224
00:15:11,032 --> 00:15:13,919
A humpback might be
doing something similar,

225
00:15:13,969 --> 00:15:16,372
even if it is not thinking about it.

226
00:15:17,450 --> 00:15:20,840
For now, the honest answer
is that we do not know.

227
00:15:21,570 --> 00:15:28,120
Kindness, instinct, or a mix of both,
the result for the seal is the same.

228
00:15:35,722 --> 00:15:39,582
There is another reason we see
this behaviour more often today.

229
00:15:40,292 --> 00:15:42,632
For most of the twentieth century, there

230
00:15:42,682 --> 00:15:45,012
were very few humpbacks left to see it.

231
00:15:45,983 --> 00:15:49,563
Commercial whaling killed hundreds
of thousands of humpbacks.

232
00:15:50,373 --> 00:15:55,533
In some regions, only a few percent
of the original population survived.

233
00:15:56,581 --> 00:16:01,151
In 1966, humpbacks were
protected worldwide.

234
00:16:01,851 --> 00:16:04,471
Slowly, they began to come back.

235
00:16:05,618 --> 00:16:11,423
Today, many populations have recovered
strongly, and the species is listed

236
00:16:11,473 --> 00:16:13,768
as Least Concern by the IUCN.

237
00:16:14,850 --> 00:16:20,075
Scientists can now recognise thousands
of individual humpbacks from photos

238
00:16:20,125 --> 00:16:24,260
of their tails, and follow them
across oceans and decades.

239
00:16:25,309 --> 00:16:29,735
More humpbacks means more encounters
with killer whales, and more people

240
00:16:29,785 --> 00:16:31,339
on the water to see them.

241
00:16:32,331 --> 00:16:38,321
Since the 2017 study, new reports
have kept coming, from Alaska, from

242
00:16:38,371 --> 00:16:42,661
Canada, from Mexico, and
from the Southern Ocean.

243
00:16:43,361 --> 00:16:46,571
The list of rescued species
keeps getting longer.

244
00:16:47,619 --> 00:16:51,689
Mammal-eating orcas are doing
well too, in some regions.

245
00:16:52,439 --> 00:16:56,169
The two giants now meet more often
than they have for a century.

246
00:17:03,774 --> 00:17:06,208
We often imagine the ocean as a

247
00:17:06,258 --> 00:17:10,104
place where every animal
fights only for itself.

248
00:17:11,208 --> 00:17:13,498
The humpback shows us a different picture.

249
00:17:14,604 --> 00:17:16,564
Not every whale joins in.

250
00:17:17,344 --> 00:17:19,094
Not every hunt is stopped.

251
00:17:19,804 --> 00:17:22,518
Many seals and sea lions are still caught

252
00:17:22,568 --> 00:17:25,804
every year, because that
is how the ocean works.

253
00:17:26,894 --> 00:17:32,311
Here, a giant that eats only krill
and small fish swims towards the most

254
00:17:32,361 --> 00:17:37,777
dangerous predator in the sea, to
break up a hunt that has nothing to do

255
00:17:37,827 --> 00:17:39,194
with its own meal.

256
00:17:40,254 --> 00:17:42,834
Maybe it is protecting its own kind.

257
00:17:43,504 --> 00:17:47,464
Maybe it simply cannot stand the
sound of a killer whale hunting.

258
00:17:48,194 --> 00:17:51,054
Maybe it is something we do
not yet have a word for.

259
00:17:52,183 --> 00:17:57,825
Whatever the reason, somewhere in the
Antarctic, a seal is alive because a

260
00:17:57,875 --> 00:18:00,183
whale decided to get involved.

261
00:18:04,110 --> 00:18:06,747
If you enjoyed this film, subscribe to

262
00:18:06,797 --> 00:18:10,050
Naturexplore for a new
documentary every week.

263
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:12,584
And tell us in the comments: do you

264
00:18:12,634 --> 00:18:15,120
think the humpbacks know
what they are doing?

